\section*{1. Introduction}

Consistency training refers to the process of training a model to maintain consistent outputs despite small perturbations applied to the input. This paper demonstrates that gradient descent on the consistency loss converges to a minimum where the model's predictions exhibit zero variance. Consequently, the learned model can be conceptualized as an implicit ensemble of independent models sharing the same architecture and weights, where each member predicts the same output for any given input. Our results indicate that this minimization of prediction variance leads to improved generalization. We also examine the relationship between consistency training and standard gradient descent training, and provide experimental evidence supporting our theoretical claims.

