\section{Introduction}
Visual Question Generation (VQG) involves creating questions based on images and their associated contexts. While this task has garnered significant attention, it remains difficult because the visual-to-text mapping process is often ambiguous. To resolve this issue, we propose a novel framework for VQG that utilizes visual-to-text disambiguation. Our method leverages the natural complementarity between modalities by explicitly considering image regions that are mentioned in the context but not in the question. We implement this concept through a two-stage training strategy: initially, a visual-to-text localization module is trained to identify the most relevant complementary regions, and subsequently, the VQG model is trained to generate questions guided by this module. We evaluate our approach on the VQA-v2 dataset, showing that it outperforms state-of-the-art methods in both question quality and diversity. Additionally, the framework is versatile and can be readily applied to enhance existing VQG models.
