Collaborative robots (cobots) are designed to work alongside humans, making the accurate recognition of human intentions a vital component of their control systems. In collaborative environments, operators may execute tasks using one or two arms, with intentions that can be either explicit or implicit. Explicit intentions are clearly communicated by the operator, whereas implicit intentions are inferred from the robot's actions. For instance, a human might use two arms to lift a heavy object, signaling the need for assistance from a robot. In such scenarios, the robot must interpret these implicit cues to determine the appropriate level of support. Consequently, intention recognition is a fundamental element of collaborative control, and this paper specifically addresses implicit intention recognition.

